PART 4 — PRODUCT CATALOGUE AND PRODUCT CARDS

4.1 Product Card Design

Create modern product cards similar to shopping applications.

Every product card should support:

- Product image.
- Product name.
- Brand, where available.
- Product size or quantity.
- Price.
- Unit information.
- Favorite button.
- Add-to-cart button.
- Quantity selector.

Example:

Product: Amul Milk

Size: 500 ml

Price: ₹30

Action: ADD

Once added, the ADD button should change into a quantity selector.

Example:

[-] 1 [+]

Users should be able to change quantities without reopening the product.

4.2 Product Information

Support product information such as:

- Product name.
- Brand.
- Category.
- Subcategory.
- Description.
- Product image.
- Unit.
- Pack size.
- Price.
- Optional tags.
- Optional product identifier.

Do not require every field for every product.

For example, fresh vegetables may be sold by kilogram, whereas packaged products may have fixed pack sizes.

4.3 Product Images

The current emoji-based presentation should be upgraded.

Use actual product images wherever possible.

Requirements:

- Support product image URLs or locally stored image assets.
- Use suitable image dimensions.
- Maintain consistent image containers.
- Use appropriate fallback images.
- Avoid broken-image placeholders.
- Optimize images for performance.

Do not depend entirely on emojis as product illustrations.

If image assets are unavailable, implement a graceful fallback system.

Do not scrape unauthorized commercial product databases or assume access to live retailer APIs.

4.4 Product Management

Users should be able to:

- Add custom products.
- Edit product information.
- Change prices.
- Change quantities or units.
- Change categories.
- Update images.
- Remove products from their personal catalogue.

Preserve the existing custom-item feature.

A user should never lose previously created custom items during this upgrade.

